00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.319
The brief said: redesign the UX of five learning platforms.

2
00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:06.600
The research said the brief was wrong.

3
00:00:06.601 --> 00:00:13.433
This is the PTC case, compressed — it ends with a customer telling me my improvement made things worse.

4
00:00:14.182 --> 00:00:20.809
Customers didn't recognise any of our five product names — they already called all of it PTC University.

5
00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:25.724
And underneath, the perpetual-license business model was the broken interface.

6
00:00:25.725 --> 00:00:27.035
Not the navigation.

7
00:00:27.036 --> 00:00:30.564
So I asked PTC to kill four of its five products.

8
00:00:30.565 --> 00:00:35.087
Five platforms became one, under the name customers already used.

9
00:00:35.846 --> 00:00:39.990
The receipts: a million dollars a year off the print budget.

10
00:00:39.991 --> 00:00:44.693
Subscriptions from zero to sixty-four percent of new bookings in a year.

11
00:00:44.694 --> 00:00:49.074
A hundred and fifty thousand active learners migrated in twenty-four months.

12
00:00:49.075 --> 00:00:57.156
My part: product and design lead, twenty-fourteen to twenty-nineteen — first design hire, then the product's owner in practice.

13
00:00:57.894 --> 00:00:59.784
And the part that didn't work.

14
00:00:59.785 --> 00:01:04.691
Toyota used engineering images beside assessment questions.

15
00:01:04.692 --> 00:01:12.244
Reasoning that engineers needed detail, I replaced the inline image with a small thumbnail that enlarged on click.

16
00:01:12.245 --> 00:01:18.481
Toyota came back: enlarging and closing it broke the pace of the assessment.

17
00:01:18.482 --> 00:01:22.723
I had optimised for inspection, and taxed the actual task.

18
00:01:22.724 --> 00:01:28.215
The fix put the large image back, with full-screen zoom kept as an option on top.

19
00:01:28.216 --> 00:01:32.670
